the old building, not a block to the east, so it is rebuilt there in brown tile with a concrete band, round a sunken gravel court with two trees and a glass link (fit stays 78%, now covering 90% of the outlines' building cells against 81%). The plate grew to take it in, and the park's trees ring the plate; the box's pebble-faced panels are drawn in grey-brown rubble, and the pilotis were thickened so they show at the coarser voxel. Kept: the square box of precast panels faced with river pebbles, raised on pilotis on the Modulor grid over a glazed, set-back ground floor; the ring of lighting galleries on the roof round the central lantern over the 19th-Century Hall; the forecourt with Rodin's Gates of Hell, the Thinker and the Burghers of Calais. Guessed: the lantern's exact form (a steep glazed pyramid), the clerestory ring's width, the wing's height (9 m) and its court's depth."}
